+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — textwrap
+
+## Properties tested
+
+13 properties were added, each as one `#[hegel::test]` in the existing unit-test
+module of the file it exercises. Shared generators (Unicode-boosted text, widths,
+and a generated `Options` *configuration* — width, `break_words`,
+`preserve_trailing_space`, indents, line ending, word separator, wrap algorithm,
+word splitter) live in `src/lib.rs` under `#[cfg(test)] mod hegel_helpers`.
+
+The repository has no proptest/quickcheck tests, but its `fuzz/` directory was
+used as evidence per the skill; four fuzz targets were ported (noted below).
+
+| # | Test (file) | Property | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| 1 | `hegel_wrap_break_words_fits_width` (src/wrap.rs) | With `break_words = true`, no indents, no preserved trailing space, FirstFit: every output line has `display_width <= max(width, 2)` | `Options::break_words` docs ("When set to `false`, some lines may be longer than `self.width`" — implying they fit when `true`); `max(2)` because a single char cannot be broken and can be 2 columns; restricted to FirstFit because `Penalties::overflow_penalty` docs say optimal-fit may deliberately overflow |
+| 2 | `hegel_wrap_preserves_non_space_content` (src/wrap.rs) | Deleting spaces and line-ending chars, `wrap()` output equals input — no content lost or invented (any generated Options, no indents) | "Leading and Trailing Whitespace" doc section: only trailing `' '` is dropped; `'\r'` is only stripped for CRLF line endings, so it is asserted preserved under LF |
+| 3 | `hegel_wrap_is_idempotent` (src/wrap.rs) | Re-wrapping wrapped-and-rejoined text with the same options is a no-op (no indents) | Every produced line fits the options by construction; task-suggested angle |
+| 4 | `hegel_fill_is_wrap_joined_with_line_ending` (src/fill.rs) | `fill(text, opts) == wrap(text, opts).join(line_ending)` for generated Options | `fill()` docs ("complete with newlines between each line. Use `wrap()` if you need ... the individual lines"); `fill_slow_path` is this join by construction; public-API port of the `fill_fast_path` fuzz target, generalized from default Options to generated ones. **FAILS — real bug, see below** |
+| 5 | `hegel_fill_inplace_matches_documented_fill_options` (src/fill.rs) | `fill_inplace(w)` equals `fill()` with the exact Options combination its docs list, modulo trailing `' '` per line | `fill_inplace` docs give the equivalence verbatim and document the trailing-whitespace exception |
+| 6 | `hegel_unfill_recovers_filled_paragraph` (src/refill.rs) | `unfill(fill(text, opts)).0 == text` for single-space-separated alphanumeric words, `break_words = false`, no indents; detected indents are empty | `unfill()` docs: "attempts to recover the original text from a single paragraph of wrapped text, such as what `fill()` would produce" |
+| 7 | `hegel_unfill_refill_never_panic` (src/refill.rs) | `unfill`/`refill` never panic on arbitrary text/width | Port of the `unfill` and `refill` fuzz targets |
+| 8 | `hegel_dedent_undoes_indent` (src/indentation.rs) | `dedent(indent(text, ws_prefix)) == text` when some line starts at column zero, no whitespace-only lines, no `'\r'` | `indent`/`dedent` docs; constraints derived from documented behavior (dedent strips whitespace-only line content; blank lines get the trimmed prefix) |
+| 9 | `hegel_find_words_reassemble_line` (src/word_separators.rs) | For every `WordSeparator`, found words + their whitespace reassemble the line exactly; no penalties attached | Comment in `wrap_single_line_slow_path`: "We assume here that all words are contiguous in `line`... the sum of their lengths should add up to the length of `line`" |
+| 10 | `hegel_found_word_widths_match_display_width` (src/word_separators.rs) | `Word::width` of every found word equals `core::display_width(word.word)` | `Word::width` field doc: "Cached width in columns" |
+| 11 | `hegel_break_apart_partitions_word` (src/core.rs) | `Word::break_apart(w)` partitions the content in order into pieces of width `<= max(w, 2)`; interior pieces carry no whitespace/penalty; last piece inherits both; cached widths correct | `break_apart` docs + `Word` field docs |
+| 12 | `hegel_wrap_first_fit_partitions_and_fits` (src/wrap_algorithms.rs) | `wrap_first_fit` output partitions the fragments in order and each multi-fragment line fits its target width (a lone fragment may overflow) | First-fit docs ("accumulate fragments one by one and when a fragment no longer fits, start a new line"; "will not (and cannot) attempt to split them further"); extends the `wrap_first_fit` fuzz target (no-panic) with the partition/fit assertions. Integer-valued widths keep the test's oracle exact in f64 (protects the test, not the library) |
+| 13 | `hegel_wrap_optimal_fit_usize_widths_partition` (src/wrap_algorithms/optimal_fit.rs) | `wrap_optimal_fit` with integer fragment widths and arbitrary penalties returns `Ok` and partitions the fragments in order | Port of the `wrap_optimal_fit_usize` fuzz target (including its generated `Penalties` with `short_last_line_fraction >= 1`); `wrap_optimal_fit` docs: "When using fragment widths and line widths which fit inside an `u64`, overflows cannot happen" |
+
+The liveness check prescribed by the skill was performed (assertion in test 9
+temporarily inverted; hegel reported and shrank a counterexample to `line = ""`,
+then the inversion was reverted and `.hegel/` deleted). The exploratory 10x run
+(`test_cases = 1000` on all 13 tests) was performed and found the bug below;
+committed tests are back at the default count, except the failing test which
+keeps `test_cases = 1000` (with an explanatory comment) because the failure
+needs a three-way coincidence that 100 cases often miss.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test` (lib target): **161 passed, 1 failed**.
+
+- Failing: `fill::tests::hegel_fill_is_wrap_joined_with_line_ending` — kept
+  failing deliberately; it pins a genuine library bug (below).
+- All other 12 hegel properties pass at the default count and at the 10x
+  exploratory count (1000 cases).
+- Integration tests (`tests/indent.rs`, `tests/version-numbers.rs`): 10 passed.
+- Doctests: 59 passed.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### `fill()` ignores `Options::preserve_trailing_space` on its fast path
+
+**Minimal counterexample** (shrunk by hegel):
+
+```rust
+let options = textwrap::Options::new(2).preserve_trailing_space(true);
+assert_eq!(textwrap::fill(" ", &options), textwrap::wrap(" ", &options).join("\n"));
+// fill returns "", wrap returns [" "]
+```
+
+A less degenerate instance: `fill("foo  ", Options::new(10).preserve_trailing_space(true))`
+returns `"foo"`, while `wrap` returns `["foo  "]` (and `fuzzing::fill_slow_path`
+returns `"foo  "`).
+
+**Why it is a library bug:**
+
+- `Options::preserve_trailing_space` docs: "This controls if the trailing
+  spaces at the end of line is preserved, or trimmed." With the option set,
+  `fill` still trims.
+- `fill` and `wrap` disagree, contradicting `fill()`'s own docs ("Use `wrap()`
+  if you need access to the individual lines").
+- `fill` disagrees with `fill_slow_path`, which is exactly the invariant the
+  maintainers' own `fuzz/fuzz_targets/fill_fast_path.rs` target asserts — but
+  that fuzz target only ever constructs default `Options`, so it can never set
+  `preserve_trailing_space` and never caught this.
+
+**Code path:** `src/fill.rs`, `fill()` fast path:
+
+```rust
+if text.len() < options.width && !text.contains('\n') && options.initial_indent.is_empty() {
+    String::from(text.trim_end_matches(' '))   // <- unconditional trim
+}
+```
+
+versus `src/wrap.rs`, `wrap_single_line()` fast path, which checks
+`options.preserve_trailing_space` before trimming. The option was introduced in
+commit `81e11fa` ("Add `Options::preserve_trailing_space`"), which updated
+wrap's fast path but not fill's. Any input taking fill's fast path
+(single line, `len < width`, no initial indent) with trailing spaces and
+`preserve_trailing_space = true` triggers it.
+
+Per the task's ground rules, the bug was **not** fixed and the test was **not**
+weakened; the failing test carries a `KNOWN FAILURE` comment with the
+counterexample.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+No property had to be abandoned after a failing run — every failure observed
+during development was either the liveness check (deliberate) or the real bug
+above. Two properties were, however, narrowed *at design time* on documented
+grounds rather than asserted universally:
+
+- **Strict width-fit for the optimal-fit algorithm.** `Penalties::overflow_penalty`
+  documents that optimal-fit may deliberately overflow a line ("we can overflow
+  the line by 1 character in extreme cases"), so property 1 is restricted to
+  `WrapAlgorithm::FirstFit` instead of asserting `display_width <= width` for
+  both algorithms.
+- **Width-fit at width < 2.** A single `char` (up to 2 columns) cannot be
+  broken, so properties 1 and 11 assert `<= max(width, 2)` rather than
+  `<= width` (the existing unit test `wrap("foo", 0) == ["f","o","o"]` documents
+  this behavior).
+
+Similarly, the `unfill(fill(..))` round-trip (property 6) is only claimed for
+input in unfill's documented domain (plain single-paragraph words, no word
+breaking) — `unfill` cannot know where a broken word was glued, so a broader
+claim would be unsound, not a bug.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Overall the skill worked well; the workflow steps were followed as written and
+led directly to a real bug. Specific observations:
+
+- **The MSRV gotcha in the Rust reference was exactly right.** `cargo add --dev
+  hegeltest` failed against textwrap's `rust-version = "1.81"` with the
+  predicted error, and `--ignore-rust-version` resolved it. Having this
+  pre-documented saved an investigation cycle.
+- **"Generate configurations, not just payloads" was the decisive advice.** The
+  crate's own fuzz targets only test default `Options`; generating the
+  configuration (specifically `preserve_trailing_space`) is what exposed the
+  bug. The section's pointer to read `fuzz/` targets as evidence was also
+  valuable — three properties are direct ports and one is a strengthened port.
+- **The 10x exploratory run advice was validated empirically.** The bug was
+  invisible in several runs at the default 100 cases and found immediately at
+  1000, matching the skill's claim that "boundary coincidences need the extra
+  trials" (this one needs `preserve_trailing_space` ∧ trailing `' '` ∧
+  `len < width`).
+- **The liveness-check advice worked as documented**, including the note that a
+  deliberate failure leaves a `.hegel/` database entry to delete afterwards.
+- **Friction: no global case-count override.** Doing the 10x pass across 13
+  tests in 7 files meant sed-ing `#[hegel::test]` attributes and reverting. A
+  `HEGEL_TEST_CASES`-style env override would make the prescribed exploratory
+  run much cheaper. The reference honestly documents this gap (Gotcha 13), but
+  it is a real papercut.
+- **Minor reference gap:** for a permanently-failing test that pins a known
+  bug, the skill covers process-aborting failures (mark ignored) and sibling
+  exclusions, but not what case count the canonical failing test should keep.
+  I kept it at 1000 so the failure reproduces reliably rather than flakily;
+  a sentence of guidance ("make the pinned failure deterministic — raised
+  count or a `seed`") would help.
+- Everything else used from the reference (`#[hegel::composite]`, `compose!`,
+  `one_of!`, `sampled_from` over owned enum values, `from_regex(..).fullmatch(true)`,
+  `vecs(..).min_size/max_size`, imperative dependent draws) compiled and behaved
+  exactly as documented, with no unused-import or naming collisions.
